Volume = {1147},
Pages = {283-287},
Note = {III Pan-Pacific Conference on Brain Topography (BTOPPS III), TOKYO BAY,
   JAPAN, APR 01-04, 1997},
Abstract = {Objective. To clarify the brain mechanisms during the recognition of
   faces, words and figures, spatio-temporal analyses were carried out with
   event-related potentials (ERPs) and positron emission tomography (PET)
   in normal subjects.
   Subjects and Methods. In the first experiment, we analyzed the ERP
   topography from a passive habituation paradigm employing pictures
   effaces and letters to eight normal subjects. In the second experiment,
   cerebral blood flow (CBF) measured with PET was obtained simultaneously
   with the ERP recordings during a continuous performance task (CPT) in 12
   normal subjects. This task required the active discrimination of famous
   peoples faces, meaningful words consisting of two Japanese Hiragana
   characters, and simple geometric figures.
   Results. There were similar deflections in the global field power (GFP)
   in the first 200 ms in the passive and active experiments, regardless of
   the categories or nature of the stimuli. The similarity in the GFP peak
   latencies across the tasks, suggests a common time course in the visual
   information processing mechanisms during the preattentive stage in the
   first 200 ms. Right-side dominant mesiotemporal activation was seen
   during the face discrimination task in the PET results, while right side
   dominant activity was observed in the ERP topographies.
   Conclusions. A combined PET and topographic ERP study provided
   additional neurophysiological information by improving the
   spatio-temporal resolution of the analysis.},
