Abstract = {The presence of arsenic (As), a potent carcinogen, in industrial
   wastewater poses a serious threat to plant, animal, and public health,
   especially in the transition and developing countries. Recent
   developments in the field of bioremediation have enhanced our
   understanding about the microbial biotechnology processes governing As
   uptake and detoxification on laboratory and pilot scale. In particular,
   the biofilm and consortia remediation will prove invaluable in the
   developments of new strategies to mitigate this threat at industrial
   scale also. The role of microbial remediation of As in industrial
   wastewater cannot be neglected due to its proven significance as a
   natural sweeper that can mitigate As in wastewater without production of
   solid/liquid waste and reduce it to low toxic level.},
