   MAUGERI FDN MED CTR, PAVIA, ITALY, OCT 05-07, 1997},
Organization = {Univ Pavia; Salvatore Maugeri Fdn; Int Soc Built Environm; Natl Res
   Council, Rome; AgipPetroli; BASF; CON TEC Engn; DANI; Draeger Italiana;
   FIAT Auto; FKV; Instrumentat Lab; IPSOA Editore Indicitalia; Perkin
   Elmer; Pollut Srl Clean Air Syst; San Air Ambiente; Tecnol Ind \&
   Ambientali; Unione Petrolifera; Zambelli},
Abstract = {The present survey was carried out in the summer of 1995 to evaluate the
   airborne concentration of gasoline components and professional exposure
   among the personnel in the service stations from all the Companies
   operating in Italy A representative sample of 42 plants, evenly
   distributed throughout the country was taken and classified according to
   position and amount of fuel delivered into three categories: Urban,
   Extra-urban and Motorway, and in two geographic regions with differing
   climatic conditions - North and Centre-South + Islands. Air samples were
   collected in every service station during 4 consecutive work shifts of 4
   h each, at 7 fixed positions (4 on the fence-line to evaluate ambient
   concentrations, 2 at the pumps, 1 near the shop/desk used by the public)
   as well as personal samplers on the attendants near the breathing zone.
   In the case of motorway stations, 12 consecutive 4 h periods were
   monitored. Weather conditions, number of refuelling operations and
   volume of fuel sold were recorded during monitoring. Methyl tert-butyl
   ether (MTBE), n-hexane, benzene, toluene, ethyl-benzene and the xylenes
   arising from the evaporation losses of gasoline were analysed in each
   sample, compounds chosen because of their toxicological relevance. The
   airborne concentrations measured, well below TI,V-TWA values, were
   compared with the composition of the gasoline delivered and
   relationships between various components were investigated blv
   statistical methods.},
