   Air 2005), Beijing, PEOPLES R CHINA, SEP 04-09, 2005},
Organization = {Tsinghua Univ; Minist Educ China; Natl Nat Sci Fdn China; Beijing
   Municipal Commiss Sci \& Technol; Shanghai Municipal Commiss Sci \&
   Technol; US EPA; Amer Ind Hyg Assoc; Amer Soc Heating, Refrigerating \&
   Air Conditioning Engineers; Int Commiss Occupat Hlth, SC Indoor Air Qual
   \& Hlth; Int Council Res \& Innovat Bldg \& Construct; European Commiss
   Asia Pro Eco Programme Promot Sustainable Solut Environm Europe \& Asia;
   Korean Soc Indoor Environm; Soc Indoor Environm},
Abstract = {The interest in the analysis of VOCs in air has been an ever growing
   concern for indoor and ambient air in virtually every urban city along
   with the concern of people for quality life in safe and clean
   environment. Sampling is arguably the most important step of any
   analytical procedure. During sampling the following three conditions
   should be fulfilled: (1) the air samples need to be representative; (2)
   the procedure should be very simple to perform in any region and (3) no
   degradation or losses between sampling and analysis must occur. Keeping
   all this in mind this study was trying to answer several important
   questions by comparing VOC analysis in air by sorption tubes and
   canister sampling.
   This study was carried out using SUMMA canister and sorbent tube thermal
   desorption system. Both systems equipped with identical cryofocusing
   units were connected simultaneously to a gas chromatograph-mass
   spectrometer tandem. For both systems the same VOCOL capillary column
   was used for separation of collected VOCs enabling thus to maintain the
   same chromatographic and mass spectrometric conditions. For the majority
   of experiments a commercial mixture containing 3 kinds of
   dichloroethylenes, tri- and tetrachloroethylene, benzene, toluene,
   ethylbenzene and xylene in nitrogen was used. Standard deviation,
   relative standard deviation and confidence interval calculations were
   performed for the assessment of the precision of the method. Overall
   results show that the canister system gives more precise results and
   reproducibility than the tube system.},
