   AND TOURISM MANAGEMENT},
Year = {2011},
Pages = {263-275},
Note = {5th International Symposium on Green Hospitality and Tourism Management,
   Jinan Univ, Sch Management, Tourism Management Dept, Guangzhou, PEOPLES
   R CHINA, JUN 25-27, 2011},
Organization = {Technol Comm Minist Educ China, Management Dept; Jinan Univ, Sch
   Management; Georgia State Univ, Robinson Coll Business, Sch Hospital;
   George Washington Univ, Sch Business; Shandong Univ, Sch Management;
   Xiamen Univ, Sch Management; Tourism Assoc Guangdong Province; Danxia
   Mountain Natl Resorts, World Heritage Project; Balearic Univ, Sch
   Business, Dept Tourism Management; Toulouse Sch Business, Dept Tourism
   Management; Yuan Zhi Univ; Natl Univ Kaohsiung Hospital; I Shou Univ; So
   Taiwan Univ; Natl Chin Yi Univ Technol; Hotels \& Tourism Ind Assoc;
   Tourism Chamber Commerce; Lingnan Int Grp; Chime Long Grp; Hengda Hotel
   Management Grp; Amer Scholar Press; Jinan Univ, Alumni Assoc},
Abstract = {Employees play an important role in tourism service branding. Their
   extra-role brand-supportive behaviors have positive influences on
   customer-brand relationship qualities. The authors conducted two
   questionnaire surveys in high-contact tourism service firms, and
   collected employee and customer data by matched-questionnaires to
   explore the relationship between employee perceived organizational
   support, brand citizenship behaviors and customers' perceptions of brand
   trust. The results of the study indicate that brand citizenship behavior
   consists of three components: willingness to help, brand enthusiasm, and
   propensity for further development. Willingness for further development
   has significant direct positive effects on the other two components, and
   the other two components both have significant direct positive effects
   on customer's perception of brand trust. Moreover, the higher levels of
   organizational support that employees perceive, the more likely the
   individual is to perform brand citizenship behavior. Implication of the
   findings, contributions and limitations, and direction for future
   research are also discussed.},
