Note = {2nd International Conference of CIB Working Commission W99, HONOLULU,
   HI, MAR 24-27, 1999},
Organization = {Carter \& Burgess, Corp Headquarters; Natl Sci Fdn; HI Occupat Safety \&
   Hlth Div; HI Dept Transportat; Univ Hawaii Manoa; Construct Educ
   Concepts},
Abstract = {A mail survey of roofing contractors and building contractors was
   conducted on Maul in September 1997. The survey asked contractors for
   their roof safety practices and their opinions and perceptions of
   factors which influence those practices such as cost and standards used
   in local industry. Roof slope does not control practice, with
   contractors practicing a range of methods from monitoring to Personal
   Fall Arrest Systems (PFAS) on roof slopes ranging from 1:12 to 6:12.
   Over half, 57\%, work on roofs with slopes 5:12 and less. There is a
   substantial positive association between practice and perception of the
   industry standard. Contractors perceiving the degree of usage to be
   `minor fall protection usage' practice monitoring. Those who practice
   monitoring do not believe costs outweigh benefits; those who use
   equipment believe costs outweigh benefits. A surprising finding was
   that, when asked for causes of Hawaii's high accident rate, 60\% replied
   workers' compensation fraud.},
